  },
  {
    "id": "isopure-clear-protein-powder-review-ghost-clear-whey",
    "name": "GHOST Clear Whey Isolate, Sour Strips, 20 Servings",
    "brand": "GHOST",
    "badge": "Higher-Protein Alternative",
    "category": "Clear whey isolate",
    "description": "GHOST Clear Whey gives 25 g of protein per 35 g scoop for 100 calories, 1 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and 10 mg of sodium, per the Sour Strips label at the time of writing. It is sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium and uses natural and artificial flavors, so it is the opposite of Isopure on ingredients. At the Amazon price we saw for Sour Strips ($44.99 for about 20 servings), it costs about $2.25 per 25 g, almost the same as Isopure's big tub.",
    "pros": [
      "25 g of protein per scoop, a full post-workout dose",
      "Candy-style licensed flavors",
      "Similar cost per 25 g to Isopure's 1.9 lb tub"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sucralose, acesulfame potassium and artificial flavors",
      "Only about 20 servings per tub",
      "No sport certification we confirmed for this product"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Choose it over Isopure if you want 25 g a scoop and bold flavors, and artificial sweeteners do not bother you.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "25 g per 35 g scoop (label)",
      "Calories": "100",
      "Third-party testing": "None confirmed for this product",
      "Cost per 25 g": "About $2.25 (Amazon price seen, $44.99)"
    },
    "amazonUrl": "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FPPWWL2F"
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Isopure Clear Review",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/isopure-clear-protein-powder-review/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Isopure Clear third-party tested?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not by a certification program we could find. Isopure Clear has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing that we could locate, an NSF search for Isopure returned no results, and Isopure's Clear product page makes no third-party testing claim. Isopure runs its own quality control as part of Glanbia, but drug-tested athletes should choose a product listed in a certifier database."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Isopure Clear healthy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most healthy adults, yes. It is about as clean as flavored protein powder gets: whey protein isolate, natural flavor, citric acid, stevia and sometimes a juice or turmeric color, with 20 g of protein, 90 calories and 0 g of added sugar per scoop, per the label. It is a supplement, not a meal, and people with kidney disease or medical conditions should ask a doctor first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Isopure Clear contain caffeine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The fruit flavors do not list any caffeine or tea. The tea flavors are different: the Raspberry Island Tea label lists instant tea and states it contains less than 60 mg of naturally occurring caffeine per serving. Strawberry Peach Tea is likely similar, but check its label. Anyone pregnant or sensitive to caffeine should stick to the fruit flavors."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Isopure Clear contain lactose or dairy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It contains dairy, because whey isolate comes from milk, and Isopure's own FAQ says it is not dairy-free. Lactose is a different matter: isolate is filtered to remove most of it, and Isopure's Amazon listing calls the powder lactose free. Many lactose-sensitive people tolerate it, but anyone with a milk allergy should avoid it completely."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Isopure Clear flavor is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Citrus Lemonade and Tropical Punch are the flavors buyers mention most often, and they are the ones Isopure keeps in the largest 1.98 lb tub. Pineapple Orange Banana also gets steady praise. This is buyer feedback, not our tasting. If you are unsure, buy a 14-serving pouch of one flavor before committing to the big tub."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What are the most common Isopure Clear complaints?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Foam after shaking is the top complaint, followed by a faint protein or milky aftertaste and, for some buyers, a noticeable stevia sweetness. Price is the other common gripe, since clear wheys cost more per gram than creamy powders. Isopure says its newer formula has less foam, and letting the drink settle for a minute helps most buyers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between Isopure Clear and Isopure Infusions?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For shoppers, nothing important. Infusions was an earlier name for Isopure's clear whey, and the 1.98 lb tub some retailers still call Infusions has the same 25 g serving, 20 g protein, 90 calories and ingredient list as the Clear tubs on Isopure's site. Isopure now sells it as Clear Protein, with a reformulation it says foams less."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "isopure clear protein powder",
      "isopure clear protein powder with electrolytes",
      "isopure clear whey isolate",
      "isopure infusions",
      "is isopure clear third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clear Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clear-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Isopure Clear Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      For anyone weighing Isopure's juice-style whey: what the current labels say, whether it is third-party tested, how the version with electrolytes differs, and what it costs per 25 g against Myprotein and GHOST.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is-it">Is It Infusions?</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#electrolytes">With Electrolytes</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs">vs Myprotein and GHOST</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Isopure Clear protein powder gives 20 g of protein from whey isolate for 90 calories per 25 g scoop, sweetened with stevia, per the label at the time of writing. What changes the verdict is testing and price: we found no NSF or Informed Sport listing, and it costs about $2.22 per 25 g in the big tub. Below: label, testing, electrolytes, cost and alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Isopure Clear Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Clear is a good clear whey with a short, clean label and an average price. Each 25 g scoop gives 20 g of protein from whey protein isolate for 90 calories, 0 g of fat and 2 to 3 g of carbohydrate, sweetened with stevia and naturally flavored, per Isopure's labels at the time of writing. What it does not have is an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing that we could find.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Isopure Clear by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 25 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">90</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.22</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g, 1.9 lb tub on Amazon</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sport certifications found</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a juice-style protein drink without sucralose or artificial flavors, you are cutting and counting calories, or you are sick of thick shakes in summer. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are a drug-tested athlete who needs a certified product, you want 25 g or more per scoop, or you are buying on price alone, because cheaper clear wheys exist.
          </p>

          <p>
            This review covers the powder in tubs and pouches, plus the stick packs Isopure sells as Clear Protein with electrolytes. The ready-to-drink Isopure Clear Protein Water is a separate bottled product, and we only mention it to tell the two apart. For the wider field, see our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> ranking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Isopure Clear Options and the Closest Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first three cards are Isopure Clear itself: the large tub, the small pouch and the stick packs with electrolytes. The last two are clear wheys from other brands, chosen for a lower price with a certification (Myprotein) or a bigger protein dose (GHOST). Cost per 25 g uses each label's servings and protein and the Amazon price we saw on November 20, 2026.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Isopure Clear, and Is It the Same as Isopure Infusions?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Clear is a fruit-flavored drink powder made from whey protein isolate that mixes into a thin, translucent drink rather than a creamy shake. Isopure, which is owned by Glanbia Performance Nutrition, describes it as a way to enhance water with fruit flavor and 20 g of ultra-filtered protein. The brand's store now sells it simply as ISOPURE Clear Protein, while Amazon lists it as Isopure Clear Whey Isolate Protein Powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Yes, it is effectively the product many shoppers still call Isopure Infusions. The 1.98 lb tub that some retailers list as Infusions has the same 25 g serving, about 36 servings, 90 calories, 2 g of carbohydrate, 15 mg of sodium and the same whey isolate, natural flavor, citric acid and steviol glycosides ingredient list as the Clear tubs on Isopure's own site. The image files on Isopure's store also name the large tub Clear Low Carb, which was an intermediate name on the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isopure says the current formula is improved, with better taste and less foam when mixed. The protein, calories and serving size did not change between the older Clear Low Carb panels and the newer Clear Protein panels we read, but small details did. Some new labels add a line of fiber at under 1 g, and the allergen statement on several flavors now reads milk and soy rather than milk alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            The flavor list has grown too. Isopure's store listed eight flavors when we checked: Citrus Lemonade, Mango Lime, Mixed Berry, Pineapple Orange Banana, Raspberry Island Tea, Strawberry Peach Tea, Tropical Punch and Watermelon Lime. Not every flavor comes in every size; the two tea flavors and Mango Lime were only offered in the 14-serving pouch on the brand site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isopure also makes classic creamy powders such as Zero Carb and Low Carb. Those are a different product line with sucralose and an added vitamin blend, covered in our full <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure protein powder review</a>. If you want a milkshake-style isolate, start there instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Isopure Clear Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Clear contains whey protein isolate, natural flavor, citric acid and steviol glycosides, plus a fruit, vegetable or turmeric color in some flavors, per the labels on Isopure's site at the time of writing. That is about as short as a flavored protein powder gets. There are no gums, no added vitamin blend, no sucralose and no acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per 25 g scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Isopure Clear (label)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 g by flavor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total sugars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g added)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cholesterol</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Steviol glycosides (stevia)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Allergens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk; some flavors also declare soy</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The numbers are lean. Twenty grams of protein in a 25 g scoop means the powder is about 80 percent protein by weight, which is typical for a clear isolate and a little below the 25 g in a 31 g scoop of Isopure Zero Carb. Ninety calories and 0 g of fat make it one of the easier ways to add protein while cutting, which is why it also appears in our <a href="/best-low-calorie-protein-powder/">low-calorie protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one surprise is soy. The Citrus Lemonade and Mixed Berry labels we read say Contains: Milk and Soy, even though no soy ingredient appears in the list, while the Raspberry Island Tea label and the older Tropical Punch tub say milk only. The label does not explain the soy line, so anyone with a soy allergy should read the bag in hand and, if unsure, ask Isopure before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tea flavors are different in one more way. The Raspberry Island Tea label lists instant tea and states that it contains less than 60 mg of naturally occurring caffeine per serving. That is less than a typical cup of coffee but enough to matter late in the evening, for pregnant readers or for anyone sensitive to caffeine. The fruit flavors we read list no tea or caffeine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Isopure Clear Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not by any certification program we could find. We found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for Isopure Clear, an NSF Certified for Sport search for Isopure returned no results when we checked, and Isopure's Clear product page makes no third-party testing or certification claim. So we do not treat it as independently certified.
          </p>

          <p>
            That does not mean it is untested. Isopure is part of Glanbia, one of the largest sports nutrition companies, and like most big brands it runs its own quality control. What a sport certification adds is an outside lab screening each lot for substances banned in sport and checking that the contents match the label. Without that, you are relying on the manufacturer's word, which is fine for most people and not enough for some.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drug tested? Buy a certified clear whey instead.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you compete under WADA, NCAA or a pro league's testing program, choose a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database for that exact product and flavor. Isopure Clear is not listed, so it is the wrong choice for you no matter how clean the label looks.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the other question people ask. We found no published heavy-metals result for Isopure Clear from Clean Label Project or a similar program. Whey isolate generally tests lower for lead and cadmium than plant proteins, and a short ingredient list with no cocoa removes one common source, but that is a general point, not a test result for this product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a clear whey that does carry an outside program, Myprotein says its Clear Whey Isolate is Informed Choice certified. Informed Choice tests for banned substances but is a step below Informed Sport for athletes, so check the database yourself before you rely on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="electrolytes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is Isopure Clear With Electrolytes?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Clear with electrolytes is the stick pack version of the powder, sold as ISOPURE Clear Protein Stick Packs, with 10 g of protein, 240 mg of sodium and 130 mg of potassium in each 13 g stick, per the Mixed Berry carton at the time of writing. It is not the same formula as the tub with salt added. It is a smaller, saltier serving designed to be shaken straight into a bottle of water.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is whey protein isolate, sodium citrate, natural flavor and steviol glycosides, so the electrolytes come from sodium citrate plus the minerals naturally present in whey. Each stick has 45 calories, 1 g of carbohydrate and 0 g of sugar. Isopure sells them in Mixed Berry, Strawberry Kiwi and Unsweetened, in cartons of 6 or 20.
          </p>

          <p>
            Who is it for? People who train in the heat, do long sessions or simply forget to eat salt will get something useful from 240 mg of sodium. Who is it not for? Anyone using it as their main post-workout protein. Ten grams is half the tub's serving and well below the 20 to 40 g per meal that most research on muscle protein synthesis uses, so you would need two sticks to match one scoop, at roughly double the cost per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not confuse either powder with Isopure Clear Protein Water. That is a ready-to-drink 20 oz bottle with 15 g of clear whey isolate, 60 calories and added electrolytes, per Isopure. It is convenient, but it is a bottled drink rather than a powder, and it is priced like one.
          </p>

