        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Isopure Clear, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Isopure Clear if you want a light, fruit-flavored protein drink with no sucralose, no artificial flavors and almost nothing else on the label. It suits people who are cutting and want 20 g of protein for 90 calories, people who struggle with thick shakes in hot weather, and anyone who wants to drink protein the way they drink water during the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also a reasonable choice if you are lactose sensitive. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose, and Isopure's Amazon listing describes the powder as lactose free. People with a diagnosed milk allergy are a different case: whey isolate is still a milk protein and Isopure's labels declare milk, so it is not safe for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are drug tested, because we found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing. Skip it if price is your main concern, since Myprotein's clear whey costs far less per gram. And skip the stick packs if you want a real protein serving, since 10 g a stick is a top-up, not a meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            A health note: a scoop or two a day of whey isolate is fine for most healthy adults, but people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant, anyone managing a medical condition or medication, and older adults with health problems should check their total protein and the caffeine in the tea flavors with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
          </p>

          <p>
            For low-carb readers, the 2 to 3 g of carbohydrate per scoop fits most plans. Our <a href="/best-low-carb-protein-powder/">low-carb protein powder</a> guide lists options with 0 g if you need to go lower, and the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powder without stevia</a> guide helps if stevia is the ingredient you dislike.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring instead of shaking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isopure's directions say shake, don't stir. A spoon leaves undissolved clumps of isolate floating on top, and the drink tastes weak and gritty. Shake one scoop hard in 14 to 18 fl oz of cold water, then give it a minute; the foam settles and the drink clears to the juice-like color you expected.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking it before the foam settles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear whey foams when shaken, and the first sips of foam are where most of the protein taste sits. Isopure says to wait about a minute. Buyers who complain about a frothy, milky drink often skip this step. Shaking with ice and letting it rest makes a noticeably cleaner drink.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the stick packs as your main protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The stick packs with electrolytes carry 10 g of protein each, half a tub scoop, at roughly double the cost per gram. They are great in a gym bag or on a hot hike. As a daily post-workout protein they are an expensive way to fall short of a full serving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming it is certified because Isopure is a big brand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glanbia size is not a certification. We found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing for Isopure Clear. If you are tested, search the certifier databases for the exact product and flavor, and buy a listed product rather than relying on the brand's reputation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the soy and caffeine lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some Isopure Clear labels declare soy even though no soy ingredient is listed, and the Raspberry Island Tea label lists up to 60 mg of caffeine per serving. Allergy sufferers and caffeine-sensitive readers should read the specific bag, because the lines differ between flavors and between older and newer packaging.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

