          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure Clear third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not by a certification program we could find. Isopure Clear has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing that we could locate, an NSF search for Isopure returned no results, and Isopure's Clear product page makes no third-party testing claim. Isopure runs its own quality control as part of Glanbia, but drug-tested athletes should choose a product listed in a certifier database.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Isopure Clear healthy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, yes. It is about as clean as flavored protein powder gets: whey protein isolate, natural flavor, citric acid, stevia and sometimes a juice or turmeric color, with 20 g of protein, 90 calories and 0 g of added sugar per scoop, per the label. It is a supplement, not a meal, and people with kidney disease or medical conditions should ask a doctor first.
                </p>
